###  https://bioportal.bioontology.org/ontologies/MILD/MILD_120020
:MILD_120020 rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                owl:equivalentClass [ owl:intersectionOf ( obo:BFO_0000040
                                                                                                           [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                                                             owl:onProperty obo:RO_0000086 ;
                                                                                                             owl:someValuesFrom :MILD_120102
                                                                                                           ]
                                                                                                         ) ;
                                                                                      rdf:type owl:Class
                                                                                    ] ;
                                                                rdfs:subClassOf obo:BFO_0000040 ,
                                                                                [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                                  owl:onProperty obo:BFO_0000050 ;
                                                                                  owl:someValuesFrom obo:NCBITaxon_9606
                                                                                ] ,
                                                                                [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                                  owl:onProperty obo:BFO_0000051 ;
                                                                                  owl:someValuesFrom :MILD_120020
                                                                                ] ;
                                                                owl:disjointWith :MILD_120023 ,
                                                                                 :MILD_120024 ,
                                                                                 :MILD_120054 ;
                                                                obo:IAO_0000115 "A material entity, part of a human being, that is an abnormal presence or organization of material components — including the limiting case where the abnormal organization consists in a normally-present component being absent — and so has the quality pathological. Which material disease entities compose one disease is fixed by the diagnostic classification assigned to them, not by an intrinsic unity criterion. Identity is grounded in the continuity of the disorder lineage, not in momentary material composition (cf. a river persisting through complete material replacement)."@en ;
                                                                obo:IAO_0000116 "DESIGN RATIONALE - deliberate departure from OGMS. OGMS models disease (OGMS_0000031) as a disposition borne by an organism. MILD does not. Here a disease is a material continuant whose identity is given by the continuity of its disorder lineage, not by a single dispositional potential. Two reasons. (1) Roehl and Jansen show that a disposition-only account breaks on malfunction, because the very disposition said to constitute the disease is the one the bearer has lost. (2) The River Flow Model shows that disease identity persists through complete material replacement, which a single disposition cannot express. MILD keeps what OGMS was built to protect - presymptomatic disease, and the separation of disease from diagnosis and from manifestation - via the pathological quality every material disease entity has (pathological disorder, its exact synonym, was the class MILD_120022 until 2026-10-30), and via the diagnosis and classification layers. OGMS_0000031 is therefore related but NOT equivalent, and is deliberately not asserted as a parent of this class."@en ;
                                                                dc:description "A material entity, part of a human being, that is an abnormal presence or organization of material components — including the limiting case where the abnormal organization consists in a normally-present component being absent — and so has the quality pathological. Which material disease entities compose one disease is fixed by the diagnostic classification assigned to them, not by an intrinsic unity criterion. Identity is grounded in the continuity of the disorder lineage, not in momentary material composition (cf. a river persisting through complete material replacement)."@en ;
                                                                oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym "pathological disorder"@en ;
                                                                rdfs:comment "A material disease entity should not be identified with a merely voluntary, transient, and immediately reversible functional deviation. In substance use disorder cases, the candidate material disease entity is the relatively stable neurobiological or neuropsychological organization that grounds impaired control, craving, tolerance, withdrawal tendency, or harmful recurrence, not a single act of substance use."@en ,
                                                                             "A named disease expression may be used for a material disease entity only when it denotes an individual pathological organization in a patient, not merely a diagnostic category, criterion set, behaviour, or social/legal classification. For example, a material substance use disorder case would require a stable pathological neuropsychological organization grounding impaired control and disease course processes; the substance itself and isolated use events are not material disease entities."@en ,
                                                                             "ExO_0000079 is reused as a comparison anchor rather than as the MILD disease definition: material disease entity may involve abnormal functioning, abnormal localization of normal functioning, or abnormal localization/organization of constituents relative to relevant members of the same species. Species-comparative abnormality is not sufficient by itself, because rare variation is not automatically pathological."@en ,
                                                                             "Pathologicity is asserted, never inferred: a material entity is a material disease entity because medicine classifies it as pathological (the quality pathological, MILD_120102). MILD lays down no criteria, rules or inferences for it, because what is pathological can depend on context."@en ;
                                                                rdfs:label "material disease entity"@en ;
                                                                skos:relatedMatch obo:OGMS_0000031 .


###  https://bioportal.bioontology.org/ontologies/MILD/MILD_120021
:MILD_120021 rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                rdfs:subClassOf obo:BFO_0000015 ,
                                                                                [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                                  owl:onProperty obo:RO_0002234 ;
                                                                                  owl:someValuesFrom :MILD_120020
                                                                                ] ;
                                                                obo:IAO_0000115 "A process that has as output a material disease entity."@en ;
                                                                obo:IAO_0000118 "disease development process"@en ;
                                                                rdfs:comment "Deliberately no input mandatory. A pre-existing disorder as input would be false for de novo disease (the first disorder has no disorder input)."@en ;
                                                                rdfs:label "disorder development process"@en .


###  https://bioportal.bioontology.org/ontologies/MILD/MILD_120022
:MILD_120022 rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                obo:IAO_0000231 obo:IAO_0000227 ;
                                                                obo:IAO_0100001 :MILD_120020 ;
                                                                rdfs:comment "Merged into MILD_120020 material disease entity, of which 'pathological disorder' is an exact synonym (curator 2026-10-30, reviewing the thesis: \"da disorder und material disease entity synonym sind, würde ich nur eines verwenden. daher lieber material disease entity\"; chosen: \"Eine Klasse + Synonym\")."@en ;
                                                                rdfs:label "obsolete pathological disorder"@en ;
                                                                owl:deprecated "true"^^xsd:boolean .


